= Surgical Extract Cards

== Section 1: Answered Questions (Breast Malignancies)

+ *Regarding assessment of a breast lump:*
  - *a) What is the method of evaluation?* \
    Triple assessment.
  - *b) Mention the most relevant imaging investigations regarding age.* \
    Below 30 years: USG. Cause: Breast tissue remains dense, so it is difficult to identify any lump. USG easily reveals if the lesion is solid or cystic. \
    Above 40 years: Mammography. Cause: Breast density is less, so a definite lump can be easily identified.
  - *c) What are the methods of histopathological diagnosis? Which one is most effective & why?* \
    Methods include cytology by FNAC and histopathology by core cut biopsy, true cut biopsy, or cone biopsy. Core cut biopsy is the most effective. It provides tissue type diagnosis (ductal/lobular), hormone receptor status (ER, PR, HER-2) which together is called immunohistochemistry, grading, and lymphovascular/neurovascular invasion.

+ *Regarding histopathological diagnosis of a breast lump:*
  - *a) What are the techniques?* \
    FNAC and core cut biopsy.
  - *b) Which one is most effective?* \
    Core cut biopsy.
  - *c) Apart from nature of tissue what extra information you will get from it?* \
    ER, PR, and HER-2 receptor status, neurovascular invasion, and grading.

+ *Regarding radiological evaluation of breast disease:*
  - *a) What is Mammography?* \
    It is low voltage, high amperage x-ray (dose of radiation is 1 mGy / film).
  - *b) In how many planes it is taken?* \
    2 planes: Cranio-caudal view and Medio-lateral oblique view (where the axillary lymph node will be visible).
  - *c) What are the radiological features of a malignant lesion on mammography?* \
    Irregular speculated mass, microcalcification, and architectural distortion.

+ *Regarding early breast cancer treatment:*
  - *a) Mention about the surgical options.* \
    Breast conservative surgery or mastectomy. Indications for mastectomy include large tumor size in relation to the size of the breast, diffuse microcalcification in mammogram, and patient choice.
  - *b) What informations we required from the specimen sent for histopathology?* \
    Tissue type, hormone receptor status, HER-2 receptor status, grading, neurovascular invasion, whether the resection margin is tumor free, and the number of lymph nodes dissected showing metastasis or not.
  - *c) How will you treat axilla?* \
    Axillary sampling, sentinel lymph node (SLN) biopsy, or axillary dissection/clearance.

+ *Regarding treatment of locally advance breast cancer:*
  - *a) What are the clinical presentations?* \
    (Answer not explicitly defined in the provided source files beyond a heading mention).
  - *b) What investigations you will do for staging of disease?* \
    Core biopsy, USG of abdomen, Chest X-ray (for secondary metastasis/pleural effusion), CT scan, Mammography, and Bone scan (if bone pain is present, to look for osteolytic lesions in rib, sternum, or vertebrae).
  - *c) Mention about the treatment modalities.* \
    Neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NACT) to downstage the disease, followed by surgery (mastectomy), chemotherapy, radiotherapy, hormonal therapy, and immunotherapy (targeted therapy).

+ *A 50 years old lady presented to the surgical OPD with a painless solid lump in the upper & outer quadrant of the right breast since last three months with a mobile palpable axillary lymph node.*
  - *a) What is the most probable diagnosis?* \
    Ca Breast of Right side. Points in favor: painless lump, old age, palpable lymph node, and short duration.
  - *b) How will you confirm your diagnosis?* \
    By clinical evaluation through triple assessment.
  - *c) What are the pathological informations we need to get from the biopsy specimen?* \
    Tissue type diagnosis (ductal/lobular), hormone receptor status (ER, PR, HER-2), grading, and lymphovascular/neurovascular invasion.

+ *Regarding treatment for breast cancer:*
  - *a) What are the components of multimodal treatment?* \
    Surgery, chemotherapy, hormonal therapy, immunotherapy (targeted therapy), and radiotherapy.
  - *b) What is MDT? Who are included in MDT?* \
    MDT stands for Multi-disciplinary team. It includes a surgeon, radiologist, pathologist, radiation oncologist, medical oncologist, plastic surgeon, and allied health professionals (physiotherapist, nurse, psychiatrist).
  - *c) Mention the indications for neoadjuvant chemotherapy.* \
    Triple negative breast cancer (TNBC), locally advanced breast cancer, HER-2 positive cancer, and pre-menopausal women (age < 50 years) with axillary lymph node metastasis.

== Section 2: Unanswered Questions (Colorectal, Gastric & Remaining Breast Questions)

+ *5. A 70 year old man admitted in emergency with intestinal obstruction. CT whole abdomen revealed a growth in sigmoid colon.*
  - *a. How will you manage the patient initially?* \
    Resuscitation is done in the form of NPO, NG suction, I/V fluid, I/V broad spectrum antibiotic, I/V analgesics, I/V PPI, and catheterization to assess dehydration and urine output. 
  - *b. What are the surgical treatment options?* \
    For sigmoid malignancy, Sigmoid Colectomy is performed, tying the S1, S2, and S3 vessels, followed by anastomosis between the descending colon and rectum. In emergency conditions with peritonitis, Hartmann's Procedure or Paul Mikulicz Procedure can be done. 
  - *c. What are the treatment modalities of this disease?* \
    Treatment modalities include surgery and chemotherapy, managed through a Multidisciplinary Tumor Board (MTB). 

+ *3. A 60 year old male presented with right Iliac fossa lump and unexplained anemia. On colonoscopy, there was lesion in both caecum and ascending colon.*
  - *a. What is synchronous and metachronous growth?* \
    Synchronous Lesion refers to the simultaneous presence of multiple lesions in two different places. Metachronous Lesion refers to the appearance of a new lesion apart from the previous anastomotic or surgical site after successful treatment. 
  - *b. What is your treatment plan?* \
    Arrange a multidisciplinary tumor board. Perform a Right Hemicolectomy by tying the ileocolic, right colic, and right branch of middle colic vessels, followed by an ileotransverse anastomosis (end-to-end, side-to-side, or end-to-side). Follow up with chemotherapy. 
  - *c. What is the tumor marker of this disease?* \
    Serum CEA (Carcinoembryonic Antigen) level. 

+ *1. A 62 year old male got admitted with alteration of bowel habit with per rectal bleeding.*
  - *a. What is your probable diagnosis?* \
    Rectal Carcinoma. 
  - *b. How will you stage the disease?* \
    Using TNM and Dukes Staging methods. Staging investigations include Chest X-ray, USG of whole abdomen, CT scan of abdomen, LFT, MRI of pelvis, transrectal USG, and tumor marker (Serum CEA level). 
  - *c. What are their macroscopic types?* \
    Ulcerative, Cauliflower, Annular, and Tubular. 

+ *A 30 years old male had an H/O PUD. Now his complaints of abdominal discomfort and fullness after taking meal profuse vomiting at the end of the day.*
  - *a) What is your likely diagnosis?* \
    Gastric Outlet Obstruction (GOO) due to pyloric stenosis from chronic duodenal ulcer. 
  - *b) What are the physical findings?* \
    The patient may be dehydrated. Physical signs include visible peristalsis and positive succussion splash 4-6 hours after a meal. 
  - *c) Tell the Surgical option and how will you prepare him for surgery?* \
    Prepare the patient by correcting dehydration, anemia, electrolyte deficits (hyponatremia, hypochloremia, hypokalemia), and hypoproteinemia. Prepare the stomach using a 3-day gastric lavage (twice daily with normal saline via NG tube) to remove residual food, reduce stomach size, and decrease gastritis/edema. The surgical options are bilateral truncal vagotomy with gastrojejunostomy, or pyloroplasty. 

+ *4. Regarding colorectal malignancy:*
  - *a. What is alteration of bowel habit?* \
    Decreased bowel movement (less than 1 time in 3 days or 3 times in 7 days), or spurious diarrhoea. 
  - *b. What is tenesmus?* \
    Painful straining to evacuate the bowel without anything happening, also known as morning weeping. 
  - *c. What are the staging investigations?* \
    USG of whole abdomen, Chest X-ray, LFT, CT scan of whole abdomen, MRI of pelvis, transrectal USG, Tumor marker (Serum CEA), Colonoscopy, and CBC. 

+ *2. A 50 year old male presented with painless per rectal bleeding. On D/R/E a rectal growth was found, which bleeds on touch.*
  - *a. What is your most probable diagnosis?* \
    Rectal Carcinoma. 
  - *b. What are the surgical options for this disease?* \
    Anterior Resection, Abdomino-Perineal Resection (APR), Hartmann's Procedure, Palliative Colostomy, or Pelvic exenteration/eventeration. 
  - *c. What is Duke's staging?* \
    Stage A: Malignancy confined to rectal wall. Stage B: Malignancy involved the para-rectal tissue. Stage C: Lymph node involvement (C1 for pararectal, C2 for root of supplying vessels). Stage D: Distant metastasis (liver, lung, bone, brain). 

+ *Regarding colonic carcinoma---*
  - *a) How will you differentiate clinically Rt. sided colonic carcinoma from Lt. sided colonic carcinoma?* \
    Right-sided colonic carcinoma typically presents with unexplained anemia, lump in the right iliac fossa or right lumbar region, and may mimic acute appendicitis. Left-sided colonic carcinoma generally presents with alteration of bowel habit, acute/sub-acute intestinal obstruction, and per rectal bleeding. 
  - *b) How will you investigate a case of colonic carcinoma?* \
    Perform Colonoscopy and Biopsy for diagnosis. For staging, use Chest X-ray, USG of whole abdomen, LFT, CT scan of abdomen, Tumor marker (Serum CEA), CBC, and Electrolyte panels. 
  - *c) Treatment options for rectal carcinoma?* \
    Anterior Resection, Abdomino-Perineal Resection (APR), Hartmann's Procedure, and Palliative Colostomy. 

+ *Regarding early breast cancer.*
  - *a. What are the presentation of early breast cancer?* \
    Presents as a hard, painless lump in the breast, change in shape and size, bloody nipple discharge, skin changes like peau d' orange, and nipple inversion. 
  - *b. Treatment of early breast cancer.* \
    Treated with a combination of Surgery, Chemotherapy, and Hormone Therapy. Surgery includes simple mastectomy with axillary sampling/clearance, or Breast Conservative Surgery (BCS) with axillary sampling and radiotherapy. 
  - *c. Is there any role of reconstructive breast surgery?* \
    Yes, oncoplastic breast surgery is an indicated option along with radiotherapy for affected breasts. 

+ *52 years old lady presents with hard, irregular lump in right breast for 2 months measuring 4 cmx3 cm. The lump is mobile and axilla is free.*
  - *a. What is your diagnosis and name some points in favor of your diagnosis.* \
    Breast Cancer. Points in favor include older age, and the presence of a hard, irregular lump. 
  - *b. How will you confirm your diagnosis?* \
    Using Triple Assessment: Clinical Assessment, Breast Imaging (Mammogram since the patient is >40 years, or MRI), and Histopathological diagnosis (Core needle biopsy or FNAC). 
  - *c. How will you manage a locally advanced carcinoma breast?* \
    Administer 2 cycles of Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy to downstage the disease, followed by a simple mastectomy with axillary clearance, the remaining cycles of chemotherapy, and radiotherapy.